// Reflection: what a type is, as ordinary data.
//
// Every declaration here is either a **type the compiler fills in** or a
// **bodyless `#intrinsic`** (§6.4, §9). There is no `comptime` keyword and no
// sigil: `type_info.<T>()` is a call like `size_of.<T>()` is a call, and `@`
// stays what it is, the attribute sigil.
//
// **The description is data, so reading it is an ordinary loop.** A `TypeInfo`
// is a value, its `members` are a slice, and a member's `kind` is an enum — so
// `for m in type_info.<T>().members` runs at run time like any other loop, and
// nothing about *reading the description* needs unrolling. What needs unrolling
// is typed access to the value, because an accessor that yields a differently
// typed value per member can only be typed if the loop around it is gone; that
// is `#comptime`'s job and not this file's.
//
// **The structs below are filled in positionally.** The compiler builds these
// values member by member in declaration order, the way it already builds a
// `Location` for `#caller_location`, and finds each type by its `#lang` tag
// rather than by name — so reordering a member here is a change, and renaming
// one is not.

{ Eq } :: import "cmp.nest"
{ transmute } :: import "mem.nest"

// ===< Identity >===

// A globally unique identity for a monomorphized type.
//
// It is a 128-bit hash of the same whole-program key every symbol in the binary
// is mangled from (`ir::mono::type_key`), which is what makes it *stable*: two
// separately compiled units agree on it because they agree on the mangling, and
// the collision argument is the one Rust's `TypeId` makes — 128 bits is enough
// that nobody has to have the conversation.
//
// **`distinct` survives it.** The key keeps `distinct` and mutability even
// though LIR erases both (§9), so `type_id.<usize>()` and `type_id.<u64>()`
// differ. That is exactly the answer a checked read wants: the two have the same
// representation and are not the same type, and a program that stored one must
// not read the other.
//
// It is **one `u128`**, not two `u64`s. Splitting it is what a language without
// arbitrary integer widths has to do, and this one has them (§3.1): the hash is
// computed as a `u128` and comparing it is one instruction. `distinct` rather
// than a bare `u128` because an identity is not a number — nothing should be
// able to add two of them.
@public TypeId :: #lang("reflect_type_id") distinct u128

impl Eq for TypeId {
  eq :: func (self: Self, rhs: Self) -> bool {
    return cast.<u128>(self) == cast.<u128>(rhs)
  }
}

// ===< The description >===

// What a type *is*, coarsely — enough to decide how to treat a value without
// knowing which type it is.
//
// An enum rather than a string because the whole point of asking is to branch,
// and a `match` on this is exhaustively checked. `Other` is the honest tail: a
// type the compiler has that this vocabulary does not name is reported as one
// rather than as a lie.
//
// A kind made of another type carries that type's description, so one `match`
// answers both what a type is and what it is made of. `Distinct` is the
// declaration, not the representation: `str` is `Distinct` over `[]u8`.
@public Kind :: #lang("reflect_kind") enum {
  Void,
  Never,
  Bool,
  Char,
  Int,
  Uint,
  Float,
  Ptr(*TypeInfo),
  Slice(*TypeInfo),
  Array(usize, *TypeInfo),
  Tuple,
  Struct,
  Enum,
  Func,
  Dyn,
  Distinct(*TypeInfo),
  Other,
}

// One `@Name(...)` a program wrote on a declaration, as data.
//
// It is **not** a `*dyn Any`, though it could have been: the value is in
// read-only data and its type is the `id` beside it, which is the same pair a
// checked read already works over. A vtable would have bought dynamic dispatch
// on a value nothing dispatches on.
@public Attr :: #lang("reflect_attr") struct {
  name: str,
  id: TypeId,
  data: *mut void,
}

// One member of a struct: where it sits, how big it is, and what it is.
//
// `offset` is what makes a run-time field read possible at all — `member_ptr`
// is `base + offset` — and `id` is what makes it *safe*, by giving the read
// something to compare against.
@public Member :: #lang("reflect_member") struct {
  name: str,
  offset: usize,
  size: usize,
  kind: Kind,
  id: TypeId,
  attrs: []Attr,
  // The position in declaration order, which is what `member_dyn` indexes its
  // table of vtables by.
  index: usize,
}

// One variant of an enum: what it is called, which tag stands for it, and what
// it carries.
//
// `payload` describes the variant's members exactly as a struct's are
// described, and its offsets are from the start of the **value** rather than
// from the start of the payload — so a `Member` taken from here goes into
// `member_ptr` or `member_dyn` without either of them knowing it came from a
// variant. Their `index` counts across the whole enum, in variant order, for
// the same reason: that is the table `member_dyn` indexes.
//
// `tag` is what the value stores and `index` is the position in
// `TypeInfo.variants`. The two agree today; they are kept apart because
// explicit discriminants (§3) are what would make them differ, and code that
// assumed a tag was a position would be wrong the day they land.
@public Variant :: #lang("reflect_variant") struct {
    name: str,
    tag: u64,
    payload: []Member,
    // Whether the payload was written positionally — `.b(T)` rather than
    // `.c { x: U }`. It changes nothing about the layout and everything about
    // how the variant is printed.
    tuple: bool,
    index: usize,
}

// A type, described.
//
// Constant-folded: `T` is concrete after monomorphization, so this is a value
// in read-only data and the call is a copy of it.
@public TypeInfo :: #lang("reflect_info") struct {
  name: str,
  size: usize,
  align: usize,
  kind: Kind,
  id: TypeId,
  // Empty for everything that is not a struct. A tuple's positional members are
  // real members named `0`, `1`, … (§3.3), so they are here too.
  members: []Member,
  // Empty for everything that is not an enum, and every variant of one in
  // declaration order where it is.
  variants: []Variant,
  // The attributes written on the type itself.
  attrs: []Attr,
}

// ===< The intrinsics >===

// The description of `T`.
@public type_info :: #intrinsic("type_info") func <T> () -> TypeInfo

// The identity of `T`, alone — the whole of `type_info.<T>().id` without the
// rest of the description, for the common case of comparing two types.
@public type_id :: #intrinsic("type_id") func <T> () -> TypeId

// The discriminant `v` holds right now.
//
// One load: the tag is member zero of an enum value (`design/lir.md` §7b).
// What comes back is the **tag**, not the position in `TypeInfo.variants`,
// because the tag is what the value stores — `variant_of` below turns one into
// the other. On anything that is not an enum the answer is `0`.
@public variant_tag :: #intrinsic("variant_tag") func <T> (v: *T) -> u64

// The address of `v`'s member `m`.
//
// One instruction: the address is `base + m.offset`, which LIR already computes
// for every static field access. What it hands back has no type, because the
// type is what `m` says it is — the caller reads it with `member_read` below,
// or takes the address on its own responsibility.
//
// The result is an **interior** pointer. That is not new here: `&mut p.y` is one
// too, so the language has had them since `&` did, and the collector traces
// them.
@public member_ptr :: #intrinsic("member_ptr") func <T> (v: *T, m: Member) -> *mut void

// ===< The checked read >===

// Member `m` of `v`, as an `R` — **checked**.
//
// The check is the one a bounds check is: a comparison, an edge, and a panic
// block. What makes it possible is `TypeId`, and what makes it necessary is
// that a selector known only at run time cannot be checked statically by
// definition. A selector known at compile time does not come through here at
// all: that is the unrolled loop, statically typed, with no check.
@public member_read :: func <T, R> (v: *T, m: Member) -> R {
  if m.id != type_id.<R>() {
    panic("a reflective read asked for the wrong type")
  }
  return transmute.<*R>(member_ptr.<T>(v, m)).*
}

// Write `value` into member `m` of `v` — the same check, the other direction.
@public member_write :: func <T, R> (v: *mut T, m: Member, value: R) -> void {
  if m.id != type_id.<R>() {
    panic("a reflective write asked for the wrong type")
  }
  transmute.<*mut R>(member_ptr.<T>(v, m)).* = value
}

// ===< `Any` >===

// The one thing every type can answer: which type it is.
//
// A trait with a blanket impl, over the `{ data, vtable }` machinery a `*dyn
// Trait` already is (§7b) — nothing new is built here. `self` is a pointer
// because that is what a trait object can promise: erasing the type erases its
// size, so the receiver cannot be laid out by value.
@public Any :: trait {
  type_id_of :: func (self: *Self) -> TypeId
}

impl <T> Any for T {
  type_id_of :: func (self: *Self) -> TypeId { return type_id.<T>() }
}

// `v` as an `*R`, when that is what it holds.
//
// The check is the same comparison `member_read` makes and the recovery is the
// data half of the pair — which is why `Any` needed nothing but a blanket impl:
// the vtable that answers `type_id_of` is the one the compiler already built
// for the trait object.
@public downcast :: func <R> (v: *dyn Any) -> Option.<*R> {
  if v.type_id_of() != type_id.<R>() { return .none }
  return .some(transmute.<*R>(transmute.<Dyn>(v).data))
}

// The two words a trait object is (§7b). Private, for the same reason `Raw` is
// private in `core/c`: a program that wants the value back writes `downcast`.
Dyn :: struct { data: *mut void, vtable: *mut void }

// ===< Attributes >===

// The `A` written on `m`, when one was.
//
// The same shape the checked read has, for the same reason: what is stored is
// bytes plus an identity, and handing them back as an `A` is a comparison and a
// reinterpretation.
@public attr_of :: func <A> (attrs: []Attr) -> Option.<A> {
  let want: TypeId := type_id.<A>()
  let mut i: usize := 0
  while i < attrs.len() {
    if attrs[i].id == want {
      return .some(transmute.<*A>(attrs[i].data).*)
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return .none
}

// The member of `T` called `name`, or nothing.
@public member_of :: func <T> (name: str) -> Option.<Member> {
  let info: TypeInfo := type_info.<T>()
  let mut i: usize := 0
  while i < info.members.len() {
    if info.members[i].name == name { return .some(info.members[i]) }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return .none
}

// Which variant `v` holds.
//
// A search rather than an index, because a tag is not a position (see
// [`Variant`]). The list is short — an enum with a hundred variants is a rare
// thing and this is a walk over read-only data.
@public variant_of :: func <T> (v: *T) -> Option.<Variant> {
    let info: TypeInfo := type_info.<T>()
    let tag: u64 := variant_tag.<T>(v)
    let mut i: usize := 0
    while i < info.variants.len() {
        if info.variants[i].tag == tag { return .some(info.variants[i]) }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return .none
}
